Compare and contrast co-insurance and Yearly Renewable Term (YRT) as life reinsurance methods, with specific reference to:
(a) what is ceded to the reinsurer in each case;
(b) the balance sheet and cash flow implications for the cedant insurer;
(c) which method is more appropriate for a pure term assurance product and why;
(d) which method is more appropriate for a 20-year participating endowment product and why; and (e) how the IFRS 17 accounting treatment of reinsurance assets differs between the two methods.

NIIRA 2025’s ₦35 billion minimum capital requirement for Nigerian reinsurers is intended to transform the domestic reinsurance market from a fragmented, undercapitalised sector into a credible first-line market capable of retaining a significantly larger proportion of Nigerian insurance risk. Critically evaluate:
(a) the rationale for the recapitalisation requirement and the market development objectives it serves;
(b) the likely consolidation dynamics in the domestic reinsurance market as smaller reinsurers face the capital threshold;
(c) the implications for Nigerian life insurers of a reduced number of, but better-capitalised, domestic reinsurance counterparties; and (d) the regulatory and competitive tensions between the domestic cession obligation and the need to access international reinsurance capacity for very large individual life risks.
